      <!-- INTRO -->
      <p><strong>Tilt valve vs vertical valve aerosol</strong> selection determines how a can's spray is actuated and, in many cases, the spray angle it can deliver. A vertical valve opens only when pressed straight down along the can's central axis, while a tilt valve opens when its actuator stem is tilted off-axis, allowing directional or angled spray without repositioning the can. This distinction matters for product developers because valve type is decided early in development — it constrains actuator design, gasket geometry, and manufacturing tooling — and choosing incorrectly can mean redesigning the actuator system late in the product development cycle.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 1 -->
      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>Tilt Valve vs Vertical Valve: The Core Mechanism</h2>
        <p>Both valve types share the same basic purpose — sealing the can under pressure until actuated, then opening a controlled path for product and propellant to exit through the actuator — but they differ in the direction of mechanical force required to break that seal. A vertical valve's stem moves straight down into the valve body, compressing a spring and unseating a gasket to open a fixed orifice path. A tilt valve's stem is instead designed to pivot at an angle, so pressing the actuator sideways or at a designed angle unseats the internal seal, a mechanically more complex arrangement that gives actuator designers more flexibility in spray direction.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Vertical valve:</strong> Actuates via straight downward stem movement along the can's central axis</li>
          <li><strong>Tilt valve:</strong> Actuates via angled or pivoting stem movement, enabling off-axis spray direction</li>
          <li><strong>Shared function:</strong> Both seal against propellant pressure until deliberately actuated</li>
          <li><strong>Design impact:</strong> Valve type constrains compatible actuator geometry and manufacturing tooling</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Understanding this mechanical distinction is foundational before diving into how each valve performs in practice — for a broader primer on valve and gasket fundamentals across aerosol systems, see our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" target="_top">aerosol valve and gasket guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- DIAGRAM IMAGE -->
      <img
