        alt="Tilt valve vs vertical valve aerosol mechanism cross-section showing internal spring and gasket components — Global Aerosols"
        width="900" height="500"
        loading="lazy">
      <p class="img-caption">Cross-section view of a tilt valve mechanism, showing the internal spring and gasket geometry that permits off-axis actuation.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>How Tilt Valves Work</h2>
        <p>A tilt valve's stem sits in a valve housing engineered to allow controlled pivoting rather than purely axial travel, so that pressing the actuator at an angle displaces the stem enough to unseat the internal gasket seal and open the flow path. This mechanism is what allows an actuator to be shaped and oriented for angled or directional spray without requiring the user to tip the entire can. Because the sealing geometry must accommodate off-axis movement while still maintaining a reliable pressure seal when at rest, tilt valves are inherently more complex to engineer and manufacture consistently than vertical valves.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          The added mechanical complexity of a tilt valve is not just an engineering detail — it directly increases the importance of gasket material selection, dimensional tolerance, and crimping quality control, since the sealing margin for error is tighter than in a simpler vertical valve design.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>How Vertical Valves Work</h2>
        <p>A vertical valve is the more mechanically straightforward of the two designs: the actuator stem travels straight down into the valve body when pressed, compressing the internal spring and unseating a simple gasket seal to open the orifice path directly beneath the point of pressure. Because the motion is purely axial, the sealing geometry is simpler to manufacture consistently, gasket wear patterns are more predictable, and vertical valves remain the default choice across the majority of aerosol product categories — household, industrial, and much of personal care.</p>
        <h3>Why Vertical Remains the Default</h3>
        <p>Vertical valves benefit from decades of established tooling, supplier familiarity, and manufacturing process control across the industry, which generally makes them lower-cost and lower-risk to qualify for a new product compared to a tilt valve system. Unless a product specifically requires the directional actuation benefit a tilt valve provides, the simpler vertical valve is usually the more defensible default engineering choice, a principle consistent with the broader actuator and valve selection logic covered in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-actuators-101" target="_top">Aerosol Actuators 101 guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

