      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="section-4">
        <h2>Selection Criteria by Use Case</h2>
        <p>Choosing between a tilt valve and a vertical valve should be driven by the product's required spray direction, ergonomic use pattern, and category convention, decided early since it determines can, valve cup, and actuator tooling that are expensive to change later. Personal care products such as certain antiperspirant and body spray formats sometimes use tilt valves specifically to support angled underarm or directional application without requiring the user to tilt the can itself, while most industrial, household, and general personal care aerosols default to vertical valves for their manufacturing simplicity and reliability.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Factor</th>
                <th>Vertical Valve</th>
                <th>Tilt Valve</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Actuation direction</td><td>Straight down, axial</td><td>Angled or pivoting, off-axis</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Mechanical complexity</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Typical use case</td><td>General industrial, household, most personal care</td><td>Directional spray products, select personal care formats</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Manufacturing tooling familiarity</td><td>Widely established across suppliers</td><td>More specialized, fewer qualified suppliers</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sealing tolerance sensitivity</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher — requires tighter QC</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Beyond ergonomics, valve type selection should also be validated against the propellant and formulation viscosity in use, since valve orifice and spring characteristics interact with product flow behavior — a consideration explored more broadly in our overview of <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosols" target="_top">aerosol propellant and valve systems</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>Gasket &amp; Sealing Considerations</h2>
        <p>Gasket material selection for both valve types is governed primarily by chemical compatibility with the propellant and formulation — EPDM, Buna-N, and other elastomer families are selected based on resistance to swelling or degradation from hydrocarbon propellants, DME, or aqueous formulations, regardless of whether the valve is tilt or vertical. Propellant vapor pressure and chemical property data from sources such as the <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem chemical database</a> informs which elastomer families are suitable for a given propellant system. What differs between the two valve types is the gasket's physical geometry and the sealing surface design required to accommodate each valve's actuation mechanics, since a tilt valve gasket must maintain a reliable seal despite off-axis stem movement.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Never assume a gasket material qualified for a vertical valve will perform identically in a tilt valve configuration without dedicated compatibility and leak testing — the different sealing geometry can change wear patterns and long-term seal reliability even when the elastomer chemistry itself is unchanged.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Manufacturing &amp; Compatibility Factors</h2>
        <p>Because valve type affects can, valve cup, and actuator tooling, manufacturers must confirm supplier capability and component compatibility early in development rather than treating valve selection as a late-stage decision. Tilt valves in particular require verifying that the chosen actuator, valve cup crimp specification, and gasket geometry are all qualified together as a matched system, since mismatched tolerances between suppliers are a more common source of field failure in tilt valve products than in the simpler vertical valve configuration. Finished valve assemblies of either type are typically validated against recognized aerosol testing methodology such as <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM aerosol product standards</a>, which cover sampling and performance testing relevant to valve function and spray delivery.</p>
        <p>Products intended for inverted spraying — such as certain foam or coating products designed to dispense upside down — require a dedicated inverted-use valve and dip tube design rather than a standard vertical or tilt valve used upside down, since dip tube positioning assumes a specific can orientation during use. How valve and dip tube design interact more broadly is covered in our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/how-aerosol-cans-work" target="_top">how aerosol cans work</a>, which explains the full propellant, valve, and spray dynamics chain from can to actuator.</p>
        <p>Teams switching an existing product from vertical to tilt valve format, or vice versa, should treat the change as a full requalification rather than a drop-in component swap, since spray performance, dose consistency, and leak rate can all shift even when the can and propellant system remain otherwise unchanged. Building valve type decisions into the formal development gate process — alongside propellant selection and actuator design — helps avoid late-stage rework, a workflow discipline covered more broadly in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" target="_top">Aerosol Manufacturing 101 guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between a tilt valve and a vertical valve aerosol?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">A vertical valve actuates only when pressed straight down along the can's central axis, while a tilt valve actuates when its actuator stem is tilted at an angle from vertical, typically to direct spray sideways or at a specific angle without requiring the user to reposition the can. The mechanical difference lies in the valve stem and spring geometry, which in a tilt valve allows off-axis movement to open the valve seal.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why would a product use a tilt valve instead of a vertical valve?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Tilt valves are selected when the actuator needs to direct spray at an angle relative to the can body, such as underarm application in some antiperspirant formats, or where ergonomic actuator design benefits from off-axis triggering. They allow product designers more flexibility in actuator shape and spray angle without changing the fundamental vertical orientation of the can during use.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can a vertical valve be used in an inverted aerosol can?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Standard vertical valves are generally designed for upright use, where the dip tube draws liquid product from the bottom of the can. Products intended for inverted spraying, such as some foam or paint products designed to be sprayed upside down, typically require a specific inverted-use valve design with a dip tube and valve geometry engineered for that orientation, rather than a standard vertical valve used upside down.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does valve type affect spray pattern or only actuation direction?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Valve type primarily governs how the valve is actuated (straight-down vs tilted) rather than the spray pattern itself, which is more strongly influenced by the actuator's orifice geometry and mechanical breakup design. However, valve and actuator are engineered as a matched system, so valve type selection does constrain which actuator designs are compatible and therefore indirectly influences achievable spray patterns.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Are tilt valves more prone to leakage than vertical valves?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Not inherently, but tilt valves have a more complex sealing geometry to accommodate off-axis actuation, which means gasket material selection, manufacturing tolerance, and valve cup crimping quality are all more critical to maintaining a reliable seal compared to a simpler vertical valve design. Properly manufactured and tested tilt valves perform reliably, but the engineering margin for error is generally tighter than with vertical valves.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How is valve type selected during aerosol product development?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Valve type selection is typically driven by the intended actuator design, spray direction requirement, and product category convention, and is decided early in development since it affects can, valve cup, and actuator tooling. Formulators and packaging engineers evaluate ergonomic use pattern, required spray angle, and compatibility with the chosen propellant and formulation viscosity before finalizing valve type.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do tilt valves and vertical valves use different gasket materials?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Gasket material selection is driven primarily by chemical compatibility with the propellant and formulation rather than by valve type itself, so the same gasket material families (such as EPDM or Buna-N) are used across both valve types. However, the gasket's physical geometry and sealing surface design does differ between tilt and vertical valves to accommodate their different actuation mechanics.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Which industries most commonly use tilt valve aerosols?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Tilt valves are common in personal care categories such as some antiperspirant and body spray formats where an angled actuator improves application ergonomics, and in select household and automotive products where a directional spray angle is functionally useful. Vertical valves remain the more common default across most industrial, household, and personal care aerosol categories overall.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

